TITLE:: RCMarkov
summary:: Markov chains: transition matrices, their stationary distribution and entropy, a matrix from a protocol, the coupled mechanism of Analogique
categories:: Libraries>reCurrent
related:: Classes/RCLaws, Classes/RCSieve, Classes/RCCrawlerMoves

DESCRIPTION::
The Markovian stochastic music of emphasis::Formalized Music:: (ch. II-III). A matrix is an Array of rows, row i holding the probabilities of the states entered from state i (rows sum to 1). Xenakis writes his matrices by columns: his (β), X→X 0.85 / X→Y 0.15, Y→X 0.4 / Y→Y 0.6 (p. 82), is code::[[0.85, 0.15], [0.4, 0.6]]:: here.

code::
~beta = [[0.85, 0.15], [0.4, 0.6]];
RCMarkov.stationary(~beta);        // [0.727, 0.273] (p. 85)
RCMarkov.entropy(~beta);           // 0.708 bits (p. 86 prints 0.707)
RCMarkov.next(~beta, 0);           // 0 or 1
RCMarkov.fromProtocol("ABABBBABAABABABABBBBABAABABBAABABBABAAABABBAABBABBA", [\A, \B]).counts;   // [[6, 17], [17, 10]] (p. 73-74)
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: next
The next state from strong::state:: by the matrix (the row normalised as drawn).

METHOD:: stationary
The stationary distribution, by iteration from the uniform one (p. 85-86: the fixed probability vector).

METHOD:: rowEntropy
The entropy in bits of one row of probabilities.

METHOD:: entropy
The mean entropy of the chain in bits: each row's entropy weighted by the stationary probability of its state (p. 86).

METHOD:: fromProtocol
The matrix a protocol implies (p. 73-74): strong::protocol:: a String (one character per state) or an Array of Symbols, strong::states:: their names in matrix order (the sorted distinct ones when nil). Returns code::(states:, mtp:, counts:):: — the names, the row-stochastic matrix (a state never left gets a uniform row) and the transition counts.

METHOD:: isStochastic
Every row sums to one within strong::tol::, every entry non-negative.

METHOD:: normalize
Rows scaled to sum to one (an empty row becomes uniform).

METHOD:: uniform
The n × n matrix of equal probabilities.

METHOD:: coupledStep
One step of a coupled mechanism (Analogique A et B, p. 83-84: "f0 standing before two urns (α) and (β)... with a probability equal to 1/2"). strong::vars:: is an Array of variable Events:
code::
(key: \f, state: 0, mtps: (alpha: ~alpha, beta: ~beta), couplings: (g: [\beta, \alpha], d: [\alpha, \beta]))
::
For each variable the other variables' states propose matrices through its strong::couplings:: (the state of variable g, as an index into g's table, names one of f's strong::mtps::); one proposal is chosen at random and the next state is drawn from that matrix's row of the current state. Without couplings strong::mtp:: names the matrix, else the only one (the first by name). Every variable steps from the previous states. Writes code::state:: and code::mtp_used:: into each variable and returns the names used, in the variables' order. A variable whose matrix cannot be found keeps its state (reported through link::Classes/RCLog::).

EXAMPLES::
code::
// Analogique A et B: three variables, the same pair of matrices each, Xenakis' couplings (p. 83)
(
var alpha = [[0.2, 0.8], [0.8, 0.2]], beta = [[0.85, 0.15], [0.4, 0.6]];
~vars = [
	(key: \f, state: 0, mtps: (alpha: alpha, beta: beta), couplings: (d: [\alpha, \beta], g: [\beta, \alpha])),
	(key: \g, state: 0, mtps: (alpha: alpha, beta: beta), couplings: (f: [\alpha, \beta], d: [\alpha, \beta])),
	(key: \d, state: 0, mtps: (alpha: alpha, beta: beta), couplings: (f: [\alpha, \beta], g: [\alpha, \beta]))
];
20.collect { RCMarkov.coupledStep(~vars); ~vars.collect(_[\state]) };   // the screens' letters, as f g d
)
::
